const fs=require('node:fs'),path=require('node:path');
const root=path.join(__dirname,'..'),today='2026-10-24';
const read=f=>JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(root,f),'utf8'));
const write=(f,v)=>fs.writeFileSync(path.join(root,f),JSON.stringify(v,null,2)+'\n','utf8');
const details=read('data/destination-guide-details.json'),coverage=read('data/coverage-manifest.json'),routesData=read('data/place-routes.json'),photos=read('data/photo-audit.json'),commons=read('data/commons-photo-research.json'),manifest=read('data/coverage-manifest.json');
const stories={
'furano-in-bloom':`Furano's summer is a working landscape turned into a flower calendar. Farm Tomita began growing lavender for fragrance, and its traditional field survived the decline of lavender production after a photograph brought visitors to the farm in the 1970s. That history changes how the rows feel: they are not a decorative set, but the remnant of a crop that found a second life through travel. Walk first through the traditional lavender slope, then follow the farm paths past the distillery and seasonal beds; the scent shifts as the wind crosses the basin. Lavender East lies about four kilometres away and is a separate outing, so it needs a car, taxi or planned transfer rather than an assumed stroll. Late June to early August is the main lavender window, with different varieties and other flowers extending colour beyond peak weeks. The surrounding Tokachi range gives the fields their horizon. Allow half a day for the main farm, or a full day if adding nearby sites; confirm bloom reports and summer train dates before setting out.`,
'the-blue-of-biei':`The Blue Pond is not a natural lake discovered unchanged in the forest. It formed during a 1980s erosion-control project, when a dam was built to protect Biei from volcanic mudflows. Mineral-rich water from the Shirahige Falls area enters the Biei River, and fine particles scatter light so the pond shifts between milky turquoise and deep cobalt. The pale larch trunks are dead trees, their stillness part of the scene rather than a planted display. Start at the designated paths and viewing area, then continue to Blue River Bridge for a different look at the blue water and falls; these are separate stops, so allow for the drive or bus connection. Biei's famous patchwork fields lie farther away and are working farmland: view them from public roads and never enter planted fields. The colour changes with weather, light and season, and winter access can be affected by snow or severe conditions. Plan two to three hours for the pond and falls, longer for the broader Biei countryside, and check local road and bus information before you go.`,
'shiretoko-s-wild-edge':`Shiretoko is a place to look carefully and leave space for the wild. At Shiretoko Goko, five lakes sit beneath the peninsula's mountain spine, with the Sea of Okhotsk appearing between trees on the elevated boardwalk. The short raised route is designed to remain accessible under conditions when the forest trails are restricted. The ground paths pass closer to the lakes, but their rules change by season and bear activity; during regulated periods, visitors may need to join a registered guide. This is not a park where a trail plan should be treated as fixed. Begin at the field house, check same-day notices, and choose the boardwalk or an authorized ground route according to current conditions. The experience is quieter when visitors keep their voices low, carry out all rubbish and never feed wildlife. From Utoro, the lakes are a substantial excursion along the national park road, best paired with the Nature Center or a planned coastal viewpoint rather than an improvised sequence of distant stops. Allow at least half a day including transport. Bring layers: sea wind, rain and visibility can change quickly.`,
'otaru-by-lamplight':`Otaru's canal tells a practical port story before it becomes a romantic evening scene. Completed in 1923, the 1,140-metre waterway let small vessels unload cargo from larger ships; after the port's role changed, the southern edge was reshaped into a promenade while the North Canal retained more of its original width. The old stone warehouses are now part of the town's daily life, with cafés, restaurants and museums occupying buildings that once served trade. Begin near Otaru Station and walk downhill to the canal, then follow the waterfront toward the former Temiya railway line and the Sakaimachi district. The Music Box Museum and Kitaichi Glass are indoor stops that make a useful afternoon alternative when the sea wind or winter snow makes a long waterfront stroll less comfortable. Return to the canal at dusk, when its gas lamps are lit and the warehouse reflections change the view. Most of the route is walkable, but it is a town walk with traffic crossings and winter footing to consider. Give it four to six hours, and check attraction opening times separately.`,
'daisetsuzan-high-country':`Daisetsuzan is a mountain system, not a single summit viewpoint, and its scale matters to the day plan. Asahidake, Hokkaido's highest peak, offers one of the most accessible introductions: the ropeway climbs from Asahidake Onsen to Sugatami, where alpine terrain begins above the forest. The loop around Sugatami Pond is about 1.7 kilometres and roughly an hour in fair conditions, but the elevation, wind and sudden weather shifts make it more demanding than a city walk. The first snow and autumn colour arrive early here; summer brings alpine plants in a brief season. Take the visitor center as a preparation stop, check the ropeway's live status and mountain weather, and turn back if visibility deteriorates. The summit climb is a separate serious hike requiring suitable equipment and experience; the ropeway does not make it a casual extension. Asahidake is reached by road or seasonal bus from Asahikawa, and services are limited compared with city transit. Allow a full day from Asahikawa and consider staying overnight at Asahidake Onsen to avoid a rushed return.`,
'hakodate-from-above':`Hakodate's night view is shaped by geography: the city narrows to a bright strip between Hakodate Bay and the Tsugaru Strait. From the summit, the two dark waters frame the street grid and make the lights look unusually close. Arrive before sunset if you want to watch the colour move from blue hour into night, but expect the summit to be busy at the same time. The ropeway takes about three minutes from the Motomachi base station, and the summit observatory gives an all-weather viewing space; on clear days, the surrounding sea and hills are equally compelling. Pair the ascent with a separate walk through Motomachi's hillside streets, historic churches and Hachimanzaka, rather than trying to cover the whole city in one circuit. Private cars are restricted on the mountain road during seasonal evening hours, and the road closes to vehicles in snow season, so check whether the ropeway is operating. The ropeway may also pause for maintenance. Allow two to three hours around sunset, with extra time for queues and transit.`,
'lake-toya-volcanic-calm':`Lake Toya is a caldera landscape where the lake's calm surface sits beside a very active volcanic story. Nakajima rises from the center of the water, while Mount Usu and Showa Shinzan form the southern edge of the view. The lakeside promenade turns this geology into an easy walk: outdoor sculptures interrupt the shore, hot-spring inns face the water, and the long-run fireworks illuminate the lake on scheduled evenings from late April through October. Start at the Toyako Visitor Center and Volcanic Science Museum to understand the eruptions and the settlements they changed, then walk the onsen waterfront before choosing a cruise or the Usu Ropeway as a separate excursion. The ropeway and Showa Shinzan are across the bay from the central onsen strip, so allow for a bus, taxi or car transfer. Clear weather brings the best mountain views, but lake conditions and attraction schedules vary. Allow a relaxed half-day for the onsen shore, or a full day to include the volcano area. Check current cruise and ropeway notices before committing to connections.`,
'matsushima-bay':`Matsushima's islands are best understood from two heights: close to the water by boat, and from the wooded viewpoints that look across the bay. More than two hundred islands break the waves and soften the open horizon; the shapes of pine-covered rocks change with each turn of the cruise route. On shore, the compact visitor district gathers temples, tea rooms and small islands connected by bridges. Begin at Matsushima-Kaigan Station, walk to the bayfront and choose a cruise before exploring Zuiganji and Entsuin; finish at Godaido, the small pavilion on an island reached by a vermilion bridge. This order keeps the walk compact and leaves the boat as a flexible loop rather than a forced connection. If you want the wider view, Mount Otakamori and the other Shitaikan viewpoints require a separate drive or local transport and more walking. The town has deep cultural history alongside the scenery, so leave time for the temple precincts rather than treating them as photo stops. Allow four to six hours for the station-side route, and check cruise departures and temple hours on the day.`,
'ginzan-after-dark':`Ginzan Onsen is a short, narrow riverside town whose atmosphere depends on staying long enough to notice small details. Wooden inns line the Ginzan River, and after dusk the lamps catch their façades, bridges and the moving water. Look for kote-e relief panels on the buildings, then follow the lane upstream to Shirogane Waterfall; on the way back, Warashiyu footbath offers a brief pause beside the river. The route is compact, but the streets are narrow and shared with residents, inn guests and service vehicles, so keep to the pedestrian areas and follow local entry rules. Winter snow gives the town its famous contrast, but it also brings visitor controls, road restrictions and heavy demand for buses and lodging. Access is via Oishida Station on the Yamagata Shinkansen and a bus of about 40 minutes; confirm the current reservation or shuttle requirements before traveling, especially in winter. The onsen town is a better overnight destination than a hurried late-night detour. Allow two to three hours in the town itself, plus the transfer time, and book accommodation well ahead for peak dates.` ,
'oirase-stream-walk':`Oirase is a fourteen-kilometre river corridor where the route follows water from Lake Towada's outlet at Nenokuchi to the hot-spring area at Yakeyama. Rather than treating it as one long walk, choose a section and use the bus stops along the road to manage distance. The central stretch around Choshi Otaki is known for the river's force and spray; elsewhere, small cascades, moss-covered rocks and low bridges create a slower rhythm. In autumn, foliage reflects in the stream, while summer greenery keeps the gorge cool and shaded. The path shares a narrow valley with the road, so stay on signed pedestrian routes and watch for bicycles and vehicles at crossings. Bus frequency is limited, and schedules can be seasonal; decide your start and finish stops before you begin. From Aomori or Hachinohe, the JR bus ride takes around two hours or an hour and a half respectively to the gorge onsen area, with further travel to Lake Towada. Winter service can stop, and seasonal vehicle restrictions may introduce a shuttle. Allow half a day for a selected segment or a full day for a longer traverse. Check transport and road notices first.`
};
